{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"If an ETF holds derivatives that cannot practicably be transferred through its authorized-participant (AP) process, it may use **cash creations/redemptions** (or a **cash component** alongside deliverable assets). The AP pays cash on creation or receives cash on redemption; the fund can then enter, close, or adjust the derivative positions itself. This avoids requiring the AP to take or deliver contracts it cannot operationally transfer.\n\nWith **transferable securities**, the ETF can more readily use in-kind baskets: the AP delivers the specified securities to create shares or receives them on redemption. That can reduce the fund’s need to trade for cash and may shift some transaction costs and tax consequences away from the fund.\n\nThis is an operational rationale, not a rule that derivatives must be handled in cash. The result depends on the fund’s prospectus, basket and dealing procedures, derivative terms, and applicable law. Cash processing can require the fund to trade, adding costs, market-impact and timing risk; those costs may be reflected in fees or borne by shareholders. Nor does “not practicably transferable” itself establish a legal exemption or dictate a particular creation/redemption method."}}
